1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,676
A bowl of curd.

2
00:00:01,676 --> 00:00:04,572
A pot of idli batter, puffed up overnight.

3
00:00:04,572 --> 00:00:07,814
A compost pit, turning
vegetable peels into soil.

4
00:00:08,214 --> 00:00:09,134
Three jobs.

5
00:00:09,134 --> 00:00:12,432
The same kind of workers,
far too small to see.

6
00:00:12,882 --> 00:00:16,650
Every night, Diya's mother sets
curd with one spoon of old curd.

7
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:19,588
Diya wondered: who does
the work while we sleep?

8
00:00:19,938 --> 00:00:24,643
By the end, you will see how microbes
help us at home, in factories, on

9
00:00:24,643 --> 00:00:26,652
farms, and in nature itself.

10
00:00:27,302 --> 00:00:28,820
First, what are microbes?

11
00:00:29,170 --> 00:00:34,002
Microbes, or microorganisms, are
living things too small to see without

12
00:00:34,002 --> 00:00:35,108
a microscope.

13
00:00:35,108 --> 00:00:39,323
Bacteria, fungi like yeast,
and protists are all microbes.

14
00:00:39,773 --> 00:00:41,184
Some cause disease.

15
00:00:41,184 --> 00:00:44,161
Bacteria cause cholera,
typhoid and tetanus.

16
00:00:44,161 --> 00:00:47,843
But many microbes are useful
to us, every single day.

17
00:00:48,493 --> 00:00:52,095
Let's zoom into Diya's bowl
of curd, one level at a time.

18
00:00:52,445 --> 00:00:54,692
The curd is thick and a little sour.

19
00:00:54,692 --> 00:00:56,654
Last night, it was runny milk.

20
00:00:57,154 --> 00:00:58,054
Zoom in.

21
00:00:57,869 --> 00:01:01,304
The milk proteins have clumped
together into a soft mesh.

22
00:01:01,304 --> 00:01:03,263
That is what makes curd thick.

23
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:04,752
Zoom again.

24
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:08,439
Between the clumps are millions
of rod-shaped bacteria.

25
00:01:08,439 --> 00:01:13,276
Lactobacillus and its relatives,
called lactic acid bacteria, or LAB.

26
00:01:13,776 --> 00:01:16,143
Zoom into the molecules they make.

27
00:01:16,143 --> 00:01:17,137
Lactic acid.

28
00:01:17,137 --> 00:01:21,779
This acid makes the curd sour, and
it makes the milk proteins clump.

29
00:01:22,429 --> 00:01:25,329
Now let's run the whole
process, step by step.

30
00:01:25,679 --> 00:01:26,579
Step one.

31
00:01:26,559 --> 00:01:29,553
A spoon of old curd goes into warm milk.

32
00:01:29,553 --> 00:01:32,192
This starter carries millions of LAB.

33
00:01:32,942 --> 00:01:33,842
Step two.

34
00:01:33,818 --> 00:01:37,117
In the warm milk, the
LAB feed and multiply.

35
00:01:37,117 --> 00:01:39,530
Each one divides again and again.

36
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:41,319
Step three.

37
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:44,221
As they grow, the LAB produce acids.

38
00:01:44,971 --> 00:01:45,874
Step four.

39
00:01:45,874 --> 00:01:50,565
The acids make the milk proteins
coagulate, or clump, and partly digest

40
00:01:50,565 --> 00:01:51,465
them.

41
00:01:51,146 --> 00:01:53,014
The milk turns into curd.

42
00:01:53,764 --> 00:01:54,714
Step five.

43
00:01:54,714 --> 00:01:58,602
Curd is also richer in
vitamin B12 than the milk was.

44
00:01:58,602 --> 00:02:02,414
And in our stomach, LAB
help check harmful microbes.

45
00:02:03,164 --> 00:02:04,732
Curd is just the start.

46
00:02:04,732 --> 00:02:07,296
Let's see where else microbes work for us.

47
00:02:07,646 --> 00:02:08,546
At home.

48
00:02:08,476 --> 00:02:12,301
The batter for idli and dosa
is fermented by bacteria.

49
00:02:12,301 --> 00:02:15,577
The carbon dioxide gas
they make puffs it up.

50
00:02:15,577 --> 00:02:19,812
Bread dough rises with baker's
yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae.

51
00:02:20,262 --> 00:02:22,119
In medicine and industry.

52
00:02:22,119 --> 00:02:26,368
Penicillin, an antibiotic, comes
from the fungus Penicillium.

53
00:02:26,368 --> 00:02:30,125
Aspergillus niger, another
fungus, makes citric acid.

54
00:02:30,575 --> 00:02:31,579
On farms.

55
00:02:31,579 --> 00:02:36,868
Rhizobium bacteria live in the root
nodules of legumes and fix nitrogen

56
00:02:36,868 --> 00:02:38,191
from the air.

57
00:02:38,191 --> 00:02:43,651
Cyanobacteria like Nostoc and Anabaena
fix nitrogen in special cells called

58
00:02:43,651 --> 00:02:44,765
heterocysts.

59
00:02:45,215 --> 00:02:46,206
For energy.

60
00:02:46,206 --> 00:02:50,608
Methanogens, like Methanobacterium,
live in the gut of cattle.

61
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:55,851
In cattle dung, they make biogas,
mostly methane, also called gobar gas.

62
00:02:56,501 --> 00:02:59,750
So the importance of
microbes comes in two kinds.

63
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:01,716
Let's put them side by side.

64
00:03:02,066 --> 00:03:06,906
Economic importance means they are
useful to people: food, medicines, fuel

65
00:03:06,906 --> 00:03:07,903
and farming.

66
00:03:07,903 --> 00:03:10,411
Example: penicillin from Penicillium.

67
00:03:10,861 --> 00:03:15,403
Ecological importance means they keep
nature running, even with no people

68
00:03:15,403 --> 00:03:16,303
around.

69
00:03:16,076 --> 00:03:20,303
Example: bacteria breaking down
fallen leaves on a forest floor.

70
00:03:20,953 --> 00:03:21,916
Your turn.

71
00:03:21,916 --> 00:03:26,654
Diatoms in the sea are the chief
producers of food in the oceans.

72
00:03:26,654 --> 00:03:28,403
Economic or ecological?

73
00:03:28,403 --> 00:03:29,820
Pause and decide.

74
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:33,937
Ecological!

75
00:03:33,937 --> 00:03:37,935
Diatoms feed ocean life,
whether or not people use them.

76
00:03:38,585 --> 00:03:40,833
So what do microbes do for nature?

77
00:03:41,183 --> 00:03:42,682
They are decomposers.

78
00:03:42,682 --> 00:03:47,309
Most heterotrophic bacteria feed on
dead matter, and so do many fungi.

79
00:03:47,309 --> 00:03:50,954
In Diya's compost pit, they
turn peels back into soil.

80
00:03:51,404 --> 00:03:53,144
They recycle nutrients.

81
00:03:53,144 --> 00:03:58,418
Chemosynthetic bacteria help recycle
nitrogen, phosphorus, iron and sulphur.

82
00:03:58,868 --> 00:04:02,471
They fix nitrogen, and diatoms
make food in the oceans.

83
00:04:02,471 --> 00:04:06,717
Through microbes, nutrients return
to the soil for the next plants.

84
00:04:07,367 --> 00:04:08,443
Remember this.

85
00:04:08,443 --> 00:04:10,816
Microbes do not only cause disease.

86
00:04:10,816 --> 00:04:13,863
They feed us, heal us, and recycle nature.

87
00:04:14,463 --> 00:04:16,042
Now a worked example.

88
00:04:16,042 --> 00:04:18,678
Let's match six microbes to their jobs.

89
00:04:19,028 --> 00:04:20,918
Lactobacillus makes curd.

90
00:04:20,918 --> 00:04:25,103
Saccharomyces cerevisiae,
baker's yeast, makes bread rise.

91
00:04:25,553 --> 00:04:27,588
Penicillium gives penicillin.

92
00:04:27,588 --> 00:04:30,132
Aspergillus niger gives citric acid.

93
00:04:30,582 --> 00:04:32,796
Methanobacterium makes biogas.

94
00:04:32,796 --> 00:04:36,743
And Rhizobium fixes nitrogen
in the roots of legumes.

95
00:04:37,393 --> 00:04:38,859
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:39,209 --> 00:04:43,762
Many students say idli batter rises
because of yeast, just like bread.

97
00:04:44,161 --> 00:04:48,184
But the dough for idli and
dosa is fermented by bacteria.

98
00:04:48,184 --> 00:04:50,495
Baker's yeast is used for bread.

99
00:04:51,145 --> 00:04:52,582
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:52,582 --> 00:04:57,403
NEET often asks it as match the following:
a microbe in one list, its product

101
00:04:57,403 --> 00:04:58,898
or use in the other.

102
00:04:59,498 --> 00:05:00,720
Exam level now.

103
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,625
Match each microbe in list
one with its use in list two.

104
00:05:04,625 --> 00:05:05,900
Pause and solve.

105
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:12,660
The answer is A.

106
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,047
Lactobacillus makes curd.

107
00:05:15,047 --> 00:05:17,686
Aspergillus niger makes citric acid.

108
00:05:18,186 --> 00:05:20,563
Methanobacterium makes biogas.

109
00:05:20,563 --> 00:05:24,985
Rhizobium fixes nitrogen in
the root nodules of legumes.

110
00:05:25,585 --> 00:05:27,046
Here is your recap.

111
00:05:27,046 --> 00:05:28,547
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:31,747 --> 00:05:34,584
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:05:34,984 --> 00:05:35,884
One.

114
00:05:35,473 --> 00:05:38,790
Why does one spoon of old
curd make fresh milk set?

115
00:05:39,690 --> 00:05:40,590
Two.

116
00:05:40,222 --> 00:05:43,709
Name one microbe that
helps farmers, and say how.

117
00:05:44,609 --> 00:05:45,509
Three.

118
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,060
What do decomposers do in a compost pit?

119
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:52,103
Next time: moss, fern, pine and mango.

120
00:05:52,103 --> 00:05:54,000
Four plants in one garden.

121
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:55,590
How do we sort them?

122
00:05:55,590 --> 00:05:58,646
We find out in basis of
plant classification.
